{"duration": 56.569, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "A German engineer strapped a parachute to a glider that kept diving.", "start": 0.08, "end": 3.679}, {"beat": 0, "text": "Berlin, 1895. Otto Lilienthal was already called the flying man.", "start": 3.899, "end": 8.161}, {"beat": 1, "text": "His glider had a habit of pitching down.", "start": 8.581, "end": 10.641}, {"beat": 1, "text": "He flew it by shifting his body, like modern hang gliders.", "start": 10.861, "end": 14.091}, {"beat": 1, "text": "But when the glider pointed at the ground, he could not shift enough.", "start": 14.311, "end": 17.42}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He built a tower shed on a Berlin hill and jumped from its roof.", "start": 17.84, "end": 20.968}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He tried many ways to make the glider steadier.", "start": 21.188, "end": 23.432}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He and his brother Gustav made more than two thousand flights.", "start": 23.652, "end": 26.682}, {"beat": 2, "text": "His total time in the air was five hours.", "start": 26.902, "end": 29.109}, {"beat": 3, "text": "You see, a parachute is like a hand pushing back against your fall.", "start": 29.529, "end": 32.827}, {"beat": 4, "text": "The real reason was his glider could dive faster than he could correct it.", "start": 33.247, "end": 36.994}, {"beat": 4, "text": "A parachute creates drag, the force that slows a falling object.", "start": 37.214, "end": 40.713}, {"beat": 4, "text": "Lilienthal attached one to the glider, to slow his fall if it stalled.", "start": 40.933, "end": 44.55}, {"beat": 5, "text": "One year later, his glider stalled again.", "start": 44.97, "end": 47.108}, {"beat": 5, "text": "He could not regain control.", "start": 47.328, "end": 48.787}, {"beat": 5, "text": "He fell from the glider, broke his neck, and died the next day.", "start": 49.007, "end": 52.11}, {"beat": 5, "text": "The engineer who tried to survive a stall died in a stall.", "start": 52.33, "end": 55.449}], "words